IT Level 2: Network Systems

A network system is a group of computers, servers and other devices connected together so they can communicate and share information, resources and services.
For example, in a college, computers may connect to the same network to access the internet, save work to shared storage, print documents and use college software.

-What is a computer network used for?
Network systems allow devices to exchange data and share resources rather than operating independently.
Common uses include:
- File sharing: Users can access documents stored on a shared server.
- Internet access: Multiple devices can connect to the internet through a network.
- Communication: Users can send emails, messages and video calls.
- Resource sharing: Several computers can use the same network printer.
- Centralised management: IT administrators can manage user accounts, permissions and security policies.
- Data backup: Files can be backed up to central storage or a network server.
For example, at a business, an employee could log in to their computer, access shared company files and print a document using a network printer.

Types of networks
There are several types of network systems you should understand.

LAN — Local Area Network
Connects devices within a limited area, such as a home, school, office or college.
Example: Computers connected across a college building.

WAN — Wide Area Network
Connects networks across large geographical areas, potentially between cities or countries.
Example: A company connecting its Liverpool and London offices.

WLAN — Wireless Local Area Network
A LAN that uses wireless communication, usually Wi-Fi, to connect devices.
Example: Laptops connecting to college Wi-Fi.

-Wired network
Devices communicate using physical network cables, commonly Ethernet cables.
Example: Desktop PCs connected to a network switch.

Network hardware
Network hardware is the physical equipment that allows devices to communicate.

Router
Connects different networks together and forwards data between them. A home router typically connects your home network to the internet.

Switch
Connects devices within a LAN and forwards Ethernet frames to the appropriate port using MAC addresses.

Server
Provides services or resources to other computers, known as clients. Examples include file servers, web servers and authentication servers.

NIC — Network Interface Card
Allows a computer or device to connect to a network, either through Ethernet or wireless networking hardware.

Ethernet cable
Carries network data between wired devices, commonly connecting computers to switches or routers.

-How devices communicate across a network

To understand networking properly, you need to learn how data gets from one device to another.
